  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong upper level ridge is in place with 500 mb heights up \naround 580 dm and with dry air in place, temperatures will warm \ninto the 60s even with the lower sun angle. High temperatures \nwere raised a couple degrees above the NBM today as it has had a\nrecent cool bias thanks to the lack of clouds and dry soils. \nTomorrow will have more clouds so temperatures were left as they\nwere. Overnight lows are a challenge with efficient radiational\ncooling and the long nights have allowed for the valleys to get\nmuch cooler than the hill tops, with nearly a 15 degree \ndifference this past morning in some spots. Valleys were cooled \nusing the NBM 10th and the hill tops warmed with the NBM 90th to\ntry and account for the cold air drainage. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe potential tropical low that NHC has been watching the last \nfew days off the Florida coast has formed and moved over land, \nthus ending its warm core low potential. Still a deepening area \nof low pressure will be moving up the coast Sunday into Monday. \nWith the weaker low over land, the low will likely take a more \neasterly track as it will be unable to build as much of an \nupstream ridge to help the incoming trough dig. Chances of \nprecipitation were lowered west of I-81 and may have to be \nlowered even along the I-81 corridor Sunday night into Monday if\nthis low continues to trend to a more east track as some of the\novernight model guidance suggest. \n\nThe ridge slides east and a trough digs in on Monday with a \ncold front moving through. The coastal low will be well \nNortheast of the region by the time the trough moves in and the \nlow takes most of the moisture away along with it so not much \nprecipitation is expected along the front Monday. 850 mb \ntemperatures do get down below -5C Monday night into Tuesday so \nlake effect precipitation is expected. Inland extent is \nquestionable so did not bring chances of precipitation down much\npast the I-90 corridor. Forecast soundings do support \nprecipitation type to be snow, though a rain snow mix is \npossible in elevations below 500 feet. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModels and Ensembles have come into good agreement on the \nformation of a low pressure system somewhere east of the \nMississippi river late this week around Thanksgiving. The \nuncertainty is in the location of the low as looking at ensemble\nlow center locations, they range from the Great Lakes to the \nGulf Coast and even well out into the Atlantic. Enough ensemble \nmembers bring precipitation to the interior northeast that \nchances of precipitation were raised but largely kept at chance \n(between 30 and 50%). Precipitation type is even more uncertain \nas the depth of the trough digging into the central US has a \nsignificant amount of spread in the Ensembles. Taking a look at \nsome cluster analysis (groups similar ensemble members with \neach other rather than a generic mean that may smooth out \nsignals) for the late week and comparing the clusters that \nbring more significant snow to the ones that dont, the main \ndifference is the depth of the trough digging into the Great \nLakes region and Mississippi River Valley. About 25% of models \nhave a deep, negatively tilted trough leading to a more of a \nnoreaster set up, with higher snow and QPF totals. The largest \ncluster at nearly 40% of the ensemble members have a more \nneutral and less amplified trough in the Great Lakes with a \nweaker surface low leading more rain instead of snow and less\nQPF overall. The cluster trend over the last 3 runs have been \nslowly towards the more neutral trough solution, going from 29% \nof members to 39% and the snowier cluster going from 31% to 25%.",
